The potential vulnerability is related to HTTP Header Injection, also known as CRLF Injection. This vulnerability occurs because the `info_content` is directly read from user requests and subsequently stored as a value in an HTTP response header without proper sanitization or validation. If an attacker provides input containing carriage return (CR) and line feed (LF) characters (CRLF, represented as `%0d%0a`), these characters can be interpreted as the end of the current header and the beginning of a new one, allowing the attacker to inject arbitrary HTTP headers or even split the HTTP response, potentially leading to cache poisoning, cross-site scripting (XSS), or other attacks.

